const schema = [{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Service","name":"CME Content Development","serviceType":"Continuing education content","provider":{"@type":"Organization","name":"Pubrica","url":"https://pubrica.com/"},"url":"https://pubrica.com/services/continuing-medical-education-cme-content-development/","description":"Needs assessments, modules, assessment items and Standards evidence for accredited providers — written to the five ACCME Standards and EACCME 3.0.","areaServed":"Worldwide","audience":{"@type":"Audience","audienceType":"Researchers, PhD scholars, research institutions, medical writers and life-science teams"}},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"Can you get my content accredited?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No, and nobody can, because content is not what gets accredited. In the United States the ACCME accredits organisations as providers of continuing medical education; the accreditation statement names the accredited organisation as the one “responsible for demonstrating the CME activity’s compliance with all accreditation requirements”. Individual activities are then designated for credit by that organisation — “designated for a maximum of X AMA PRA Category 1 Credits”. So “ACCME-accredited content” and “a CME-accredited course” are not things that exist in the US system. Europe is genuinely different: the EACCME, run by the UEMS, accredits individual activities — live events, e-learning and blended learning — and awards ECMEC credits to them, so “accredited event” is correct there. Pages that collapse the two systems together and offer to facilitate submission to accreditation bodies are describing something that does not exist. What we do is write the content and the evidence documentation that an accredited provider needs."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do you follow ACCME, EACCME and AMA standards?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"We write to the ACCME Standards and to EACCME criteria. The AMA does not belong in that list, and it is put there constantly. The AMA is not a CME accreditation body: it owns and governs the AMA PRA Category 1 Credit designation through its Council on Medical Education, and authorises only organisations accredited by the ACCME — or by an ACCME-recognised state medical society — to designate and award it. The AMA is itself an ACCME-accredited provider, and states plainly that “those are separate functions and operate independently”. It also notes that its core requirements and ACCME’s are aligned and do not create a second rulebook. Getting this wrong is a fifteen-second tell to anyone who works in the field, which is why it is worth stating plainly."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What are the five Standards, and which one catches people out?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The Standards for Integrity and Independence in Accredited Continuing Education, released December 2020 and effective 1 January 2022, replacing the Standards for Commercial Support first adopted in 1992 and updated in 2004. 1. Ensure content is valid. 2. Prevent commercial bias and marketing. 3. Identify, mitigate and disclose relevant financial relationships. 4. Manage commercial support appropriately. 5. Manage ancillary activities. Two catch people out. Standard 3, because owners and employees of ineligible companies hold relationships the Standards treat as unresolvable — they must be excluded from planning and faculty, with only three narrow exceptions, and no declaration manages it away. And Standard 5, because exhibits and promotional sessions have to be separated from the accredited education in time and space and clearly identified as not part of it, and the floor plan is usually decided by somebody who has not read the Standard."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"We have commercial support. What can the supporter actually do?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Fund it, be named to learners, and let go of everything else. Under Standard 4 the accredited provider must make all decisions about receiving and disbursing the support; the terms, conditions and purposes must be in a written agreement between the supporting company and the provider; the supporting company must be disclosed to learners before they engage with the education; and an ineligible company must not pay any expense of the education or the learners directly. Under Standard 2 the provider must control planning, faculty selection, delivery and evaluation without influence or involvement from owners and employees of ineligible companies — which rules out selecting faculty, shaping objectives and previewing content. And learner names and contact details may not be shared with an ineligible company or its agents without each individual learner’s explicit consent; an agreement with the institution is not that consent. Where a request crosses one of those lines we decline it and tell the provider, including when the request comes from the organisation paying us."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Does CME actually change what doctors do?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Modestly, and the honest numbers are more useful than the enthusiastic ones. The 2021 Cochrane review of continuing education meetings and workshops — 215 studies, more than 28,000 health professionals — found a median adjusted improvement in compliance with desired practice of 4.00 percentage points (interquartile range 0.29% to 13.00%) at moderate certainty, and effects on patient outcomes that “probably slightly improve”. It also reports the evidence comparing interactive with didactic formats as very low certainty and states the reviewers are uncertain of the difference — which matters, because “interactive CME works better” is the most-repeated claim in this field and it is usually attributed to that very review. A separate synthesis of eight systematic reviews (Cervero and Gaines, 2015) does conclude that CME improves physician performance more reliably than it improves patient outcomes, and that activities which are more interactive, use more methods, involve multiple exposures and run longer produce better results. Both hold. Education works, at the margin, and a four-point improvement is worth having — it is simply not what a brochure usually promises."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What outcome level can we realistically report?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"On Moore’s expanded framework — levels 1 participation, 2 satisfaction, 3a declarative knowledge, 3b procedural knowledge, 4 competence, 5 performance, 6 patient health, 7 community health. Most activities reach 3a and 3b reliably. Level 4 is reachable with case-based assessment items that require application rather than recall, and it is the level most often lost at the item-writing stage rather than the design stage. Level 5 requires data from outside the activity — chart audit, registry, prescribing or ordering data — with a baseline taken before the education. If that measurement was not designed in advance, level 5 is not available afterwards, and no analysis recovers it. Levels 6 and 7 need a data source, a comparison and a plausible causal path, and the honest position is that an education activity is one input among many. We will design the measurement for whatever level you fund, and we will report at the level the data actually reach."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can you write for an international audience?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes, and the systems do not collapse into each other, so the content changes. In the US, organisations are accredited and activities are designated for credit. In Europe, the EACCME accredits the activity itself under EACCME 3.0 criteria in force since 2023, with applications recommended eight weeks ahead and six weeks minimum. In the UK, there is no credit target to hit: the GMC does not mandate a number of CPD points, and the Federation of the Royal Colleges of Physicians recommends 50 hours a year with 25 external while saying explicitly that this is not mandatory — so UK content is built round reflection and scope of practice. Canada’s Royal College MOC programme runs three sections on a five-year cycle. Australia requires 50 hours a year through a CPD Home, with culturally safe practice, health inequities, and professionalism and ethics inside that 50. One date worth noting: the agreement under which the AMA converts EACCME-certified credit into AMA PRA Category 1 Credit runs through 31 December 2026. If your programme depends on that conversion next year, confirm the renewal rather than assuming it."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"We are not accredited. What are our options?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Two, and the choice is strategic rather than editorial. Become an accredited provider: a published fee schedule, a self-study, and a two-year Provisional term before a four-year one. Note that publishing and education companies are eligible in the ACCME system — being a medical education company does not disqualify you; only organisations whose primary business is producing, marketing, selling, re-selling or distributing healthcare products used by or on patients are ineligible. Or work in joint providership with an organisation that already holds accreditation. ACCME’s own framing of that is worth reading before you begin: “An accredited provider puts their own accreditation at risk when they offer joint providership to another organization.” Expect them to scrutinise your content harder than a client normally would. That is them doing their job, and it is why the evidence pack is what the relationship actually runs on."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is your turnaround?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"A needs assessment 10 business days; one complete module with its evidence pack 15; a programme of three to five activities 30 to 40; an annual arrangement to your own calendar. Three things move a real timeline more than the writing does. Disclosures, because they have to be collected and mitigated before content development and a committee that has to be reassembled costs a fortnight. Clinical review, which is a named specialist’s calendar rather than a process step. And your own approval cycle, which is almost always the long pole: a module drafted in fifteen days and reviewed by a committee that meets monthly took six weeks. Expedited delivery is available where capacity allows and is quoted on capacity rather than on urgency; where a date is not achievable we say so rather than taking the fee and missing it."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can I see a sample before commissioning?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes, and there is a complete one on this page — a build pack for a commercially supported enduring material, with the specialty, the topic, the institution, the supporter and every figure changed. Open it above and read the whole thing, including the parts where the finding was awkward: a faculty member excluded under Standard 3, two supporter requests declined, and two barriers that education could not fix reported in the needs assessment. If you want something closer to your own work, ask and we will build one around an activity like yours — same format, same specialty, same accreditation system. Two caveats. Client work is confidential, so any sample is redacted or composite. And a sample shows method and documentation discipline; it cannot show whether the writer understood your gap, which is what the free gap review is for."}}]},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"BreadcrumbList","itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Home","item":"https://pubrica.com/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Services","item":"https://pubrica.com/services/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"Services","item":"https://pubrica.com/services/continuing-medical-education-cme-content-development/"}]}];

